---
import PageLayout from '@/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Tabs from '@/components/ui/Tabs.astro';
import Prose from '@/components/Prose.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import sivananda from '@/assets/teachers/swami-sivananda.jpg';
import vishnudevananda from '@/assets/teachers/swami-vishnudevananda.jpg';

const tabs = [
  { id: 'sivananda', label: 'Swami Sivananda' },
  { id: 'vishnudevananda', label: 'Swami Vishnudevananda' },
];
---

<PageLayout
  title="Our Teachers"
  lead="The inspiration and founders of the International Sivananda Yoga Vedanta Centres."
  description="Swami Sivananda and Swami Vishnudevananda — the lineage behind the Sivananda tradition."
  narrow
>
  <Tabs tabs={tabs}>
    <Prose slot="sivananda">
      <Image
        src={sivananda}
        alt="Swami Sivananda"
        loading="eager"
        class="float-none mb-4 w-40 rounded-xl border border-border sm:float-right sm:ml-6"
      />
      <p>
        Swami Sivananda's life was a radiant example of service to humanity, both during his years
        as a doctor and later as a world-renowned sage and jivanmukta. To serve all, to love all, to
        mix with all and to see God in all beings were the ideals he taught and lived by. Born on
        September 8, 1887, to an illustrious family in Tamil Nadu, South India, he displayed
        spiritual tendencies even as a young boy.
      </p>
      <p>
        Even as a young man, his driving force was the service of humanity, which led him to become
        a medical doctor. He served the poor in Malaysia for many years, but soon felt that this was
        not enough. Divinely blessed, he gave up his medical career and took to a life of
        renunciation.
      </p>
      <p>
        He returned to India and settled in Rishikesh, Himalayas in 1924, where he practiced intense
        austerities, found his guru, Swami Vishwananda, and was initiated into sannyas. In 1932 he
        started the Sivananda Ashram; in 1936 the Divine Life Society was born; and the Yoga Vedanta
        Forest Academy was organised in 1948 — all with the aim of spreading the teachings of yoga
        and vedanta.
      </p>
      <p>
        Swami Sivananda's teachings crystallised the basic tenets of all religions, combining all
        yoga paths into one — the Yoga of Synthesis, which he summed up with,
        <strong>'Serve, Love, Give, Purify, Meditate, Realise'</strong>. He entered mahasamadhi
        (left his physical body) on July 14, 1963.
      </p>
    </Prose>

    <Prose slot="vishnudevananda">
      <Image
        src={vishnudevananda}
        alt="Swami Vishnudevananda"
        loading="lazy"
        class="float-none mb-4 w-40 rounded-xl border border-border sm:float-right sm:ml-6"
      />
      <p>
        A disciple of Swami Sivananda, Swami Vishnudevananda was born in Kerala, south India on
        December 31, 1927. During his career in the army, he discovered Swami Sivananda's teachings
        from a leaflet entitled Sadhana Tattwa (Spiritual Instructions) which began 'An ounce of
        practice is worth tons of theory.'
      </p>
      <p>
        He entered the Sivananda Ashram in Rishikesh in 1947 at the age of twenty, took sannyas
        (holy orders) and was appointed the first professor of hatha yoga at the Sivananda Yoga
        Vedanta Forest Academy, where he trained thousands of students, both Indian and western.
      </p>
      <p>
        In 1957 he set out to carry out the instructions of his Master — to spread the teachings of
        yoga in the West. He established the first Sivananda Yoga Vedanta Centre in Montreal and for
        the following thirty-seven years worked tirelessly as a dedicated spiritual teacher and
        peace missionary, travelling the world establishing centres and ashrams. His motto —
        <strong>'Health is Wealth. Peace of Mind is Happiness. Yoga Shows the Way'</strong> — continues
        to inspire all who come to learn about yoga at the Sivananda Ashrams and Centres around the world.
      </p>
      <p>
        Recognised as a world authority on hatha and raja yoga, he is author of the bestselling
        <em>The Complete Illustrated Book of Yoga</em>. He left his body on November 9, 1993, his
        legacy a worldwide organisation dedicated to propagating the ancient and timeless wisdom of
        yoga.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </Tabs>
</PageLayout>
